 \begin{abstract}
-Low-altitude capacity alone does not ensure service resilience: an intervention must preserve useful service before its deadline margin is exhausted. We propose a Ground--Low-Altitude Mobility Manager that organizes joint ground--air states into a shared executable candidate interface. Replaceable supervisory policies select interventions using common feasibility, completion-time and incumbent-service facts, while execution checks and independent local contingencies govern their application. Four SUMO--BlueSky experiments across three contrasting transport settings examine selective air support, air failures, compound disturbances and observation--execution timing. At the meshed urban site, the fixed-objective heuristic and LLM-candidate policy both reduce emergency deadline violations from 33.33\% to 16.67\%, but leave 0.058 and 0.133 damaged incumbent services per run, respectively. Explicit candidate-information support changes choices when faster air service competes with service preservation. Every affected coordinated run restores a path after a single air failure, yet 39.06\% of all LLM-policy runs at that site remain late. Delayed observation consumes the remaining margin, and execution waiting can change the intervention itself through command replacement. The study establishes an executable architecture for comparative service management and identifies surviving alternatives, incumbent-service costs and intervention timing as the mechanisms governing realized resilience. The management implication is to secure timely, useful reconfiguration, rather than equating available air capacity or a restored path with recovered service.
+Available aircraft do not by themselves keep disrupted transport services on time. An intervention must provide useful service before the remaining deadline margin runs out. We propose a Ground--Low-Altitude Mobility Manager in which a candidate generator converts joint ground--air observations into a shared executable candidate interface. Replaceable supervisory policies use common feasibility, completion-time and incumbent-service facts to select an intervention. The executor checks and applies the proposal, while local contingencies operate independently. Four SUMO--BlueSky experiments across three contrasting transport settings examine selective air support, air failures, compound disturbances and observation--execution timing. At the meshed urban site, the fixed-objective heuristic and LLM-candidate policy both reduce emergency deadline violations from 33.33\% to 16.67\%, but leave 0.058 and 0.133 damaged incumbent services per run, respectively. Explicit candidate-information support changes choices when faster air service would interrupt an incumbent service. Every affected coordinated run restores a path after a single air failure, yet 39.06\% of all LLM-policy runs at that site remain late. Delayed observation uses up the remaining margin. Waiting for execution can also change the intervention when a newer command replaces the pending one. The study provides an executable architecture for comparing service-management policies. It shows how surviving alternatives, incumbent-service costs and intervention timing govern realized resilience. Operators therefore need to secure timely, useful reconfiguration: available air capacity or a restored path alone does not establish service recovery.
 \end{abstract}
 
